Zoom homogeneous box with GEAR sinks
=====================================

The gas box of examples/SinkParticles/HomogeneousBox, with dark matter added,
sits at the centre of a larger periodic box and runs as a zoom region
(--zoom). Background dark matter particles fill the rest of the box.

Build
-----

./configure --disable-mpi --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-cooling=grackle_0 \
  --with-stars=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-feedback=GEAR \
  --with-sink=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j

Sinks do not run with MPI. Build and run without MPI.

Run
---

run.sh makes the ICs, fetches the cooling and chemistry tables and runs
../../../swift. Choose the case with run_case:

  ./run.sh                                       # level 5 with sinks (default), 8 threads
  with_star_formation=1 ./run.sh                 # level 5, GEAR star formation instead of sinks
  run_case=local ./run.sh                        # local test: level 3, 2 threads, stops at t = 0.1
  run_case=local with_star_formation=1 ./run.sh  # local test with star formation
  run_case=level8 ./run.sh                       # level 8, 72 threads, one cluster node

Other run.sh variables: level, n_threads, gas_mass (see "Gas particle
mass"), embed, dm_ratio, delta, jeans_length, cosmological.
"python3 makeIC.py --help" lists all IC options.

What to expect
--------------

- Local test: a few seconds on a laptop (release build). No sink or star
  forms before t = 0.1 (98 Myr).
- With the dark matter, the free-fall time is about 65 Myr (0.066 in
  internal units) instead of 163 Myr in the gas-only example. The whole
  uniform medium, inside and outside the gas box, collapses into clumps on
  that time scale. The run does not reproduce the fragmentation of the
  original example.
- time_end is 0.5 (489 Myr), the value of the original example. Today the
  runs stop before that time (see "Known problems").

Known problems
--------------

- Release builds stop with a segmentation fault in
  space_reorder_extra_sparts_mapper after about one to two free-fall times
  (measured with 2 threads: t = 0.065 at level 4, t = 0.121 at level 3).
- Builds with debugging checks stop at the first rebuild:
  - with --star-formation, in cell_check_multipole on a background cell
    that holds one particle;
  - with --sinks, in the sink cell index check of space_rebuild.c. The fix
    is on the branch darwin/fix_zoom_sink_cell_index, not on this branch.
- Sinks do not run with MPI.
- Only level 3 and level 4 were run. Level 5 and level 8 were not run. No
  sink has formed in any run so far.

Cases and resolution
--------------------

The level sets the resolution: 2^(3 level) gas particles. The mean gas
spacing (27.2 pc) and the smoothing length h (33.6 pc) do not change with
the level. The gas box side L grows as 2^level. Values for 50 Msun and
embed 8:

  level  gas = DM particles  PartType2 particles  L [pc]  box [pc]
  3      512                 1344                 218     1744
  4      4096                10752                436     3488
  5      32768               86016                872     6976
  8      16777216            44040192             6976    55805

- level5 (default): time_end 0.5, a snapshot every 0.01.
- local: time_end 0.1.
- level8: restart files every 2 hours, a snapshot every 0.05 (49 Myr), or
  every factor 1.1 in scale factor with cosmological=1.
  - ICs: about 2 minutes, 6.9 GB of memory, a 3.7 GB file.
  - Particles: 16777216 gas (50 Msun), 16777216 PartType1 (268 Msun),
    3 x 14680064 PartType2 (2544, 2.035e4 and 1.628e5 Msun): 77.6 million.
  - Memory: 19.7 GB for the particles, about 30 to 40 GB in total.
  - Cost: about 2e6 core seconds to time_end, that is 7 hours on 72 cores
    with perfect scaling, and 3 to 10 times more with deep time-step bins.

Initial temperature: at fixed jeans_length it grows as L^2, so it grows
with the level and as gas_mass^(2/3). It is 16 K at level 5 and about
1000 K at level 8 (50 Msun). To keep the temperature of level 5 at 50 Msun,
scale jeans_length as 1/L: 0.03125 at level 8, 0.25 (50 / gas_mass)^(1/3)
at level 5. Cooling changes the temperature during the run.

Gas particle mass
-----------------

gas_mass sets the gas particle mass in Msun (default 50), for example:

  gas_mass=100 run_case=local ./run.sh

The gas density stays 0.1 atom/cm^3. With s = (gas_mass / 50)^(1/3):

- The gas box side L = (N m / rho)^(1/3), the mean spacing (m / rho)^(1/3)
  and h (1.2348 times the spacing) grow as s.
- run.sh passes SPH:h_max = 0.08 s kpc to makeIC.py and to swift. The initial h
  stays below h_max, and the zoom grid (bkg_top_level_cells,
  zoom_top_level_depth, mesh_side_length) stays the same as for 50 Msun.
  Without this scaling, a mass below about 47 Msun gives a smaller zoom
  depth, and makeIC.py stops at level 3.
- run.sh passes max_physical_baryon_softening and max_physical_DM_softening
  = 0.005 s kpc.
- With sinks, run.sh passes GEARSink:cut_off_radius = 0.005 s kpc. A sink
  forms only where kernel_gamma h < cut_off_radius, so the density at which
  sinks can form stays the same.
- The PartType2 softenings follow the particle masses (see "How it is
  built").

The parameter files hold the values for 50 Msun. run.sh overrides them, so
change these four values in run.sh, not in the parameter files.

These values do not scale with the gas mass: the density and temperature
thresholds of sink and star formation, the time-step limits (dt_max and the
limits of sinks and stars in Myr), the feedback parameters,
stellar_particle_mass_Msun (50 Msun, a choice of IMF sampling) and the
spare particles per cell (Scheduler block). The GEAR star particle mass
(gas mass / n_stars_per_particle) and the pressure floor (set by h) follow
the gas mass by themselves. For jeans_length, see "Initial temperature"
above.

How it is built
---------------

- Gas (PartType0): uniform random positions in a cube of side L, density
  0.1 atom/cm^3, zero velocity. The internal energy u = sigma^2 uses the
  velocity dispersion of the original example (set by jeans_length).
- Dark matter (PartType1): one particle per gas particle. Each pair comes
  from one random point, split as in space_generate_gas() (src/space.c).
  The dark matter density is dm_ratio times the gas density (default 5.36,
  the Planck 2018 ratio of cold dark matter to baryons).
- Background dark matter (PartType2): nested cube shells around the gas
  box. Shell k spans the cube sides 2^(k-1) L to 2^k L, up to the box side
  embed L. Each shell is a jittered grid with spacing 2^k times the mean
  gas spacing, so the particle mass grows by 8 from one shell to the next.
  No PartType2 particle is inside the gas box.
- Density contrast: with delta = 0 (default) the large box is uniform.
  With delta > 0 the gas box is an overdensity, rho_in / rho_out =
  1 + delta. The background then follows
  rho(r) = rho_out + (rho_in - rho_out) (1 - tanh((r - L/2) / w)),
  with r the max-norm distance from the centre. The gas stays a sharp cube.
- Softening: gas, stars, sinks and PartType1 use 5 pc. PartType2 uses
  softening_ratio_background (0.2) times the mean spacing of particles of
  its mass at the mean matter density of the box: 10.9, 21.8 and 43.6 pc
  for the three shells at 50 Msun. makeIC.py prints these values.
- Zoom grid: makeIC.py writes zoom_runtime_params.txt, and run.sh passes
  it to swift with -P:
  - ZoomRegion:bkg_top_level_cells: the largest odd number not above
    embed / region_pad_factor (5 with the defaults);
  - ZoomRegion:zoom_top_level_depth: the largest depth with zoom cells
    wider than 1.1 kernel_gamma h_max and at most 32 zoom cells per axis
    (level - 2 up to level 7, then 5);
  - Gravity:mesh_side_length: 2^(level+1), at least 32.
  makeIC.py --h-max MUST equal SPH:h_max. run.sh passes the same value
  to both.
- Cosmological variant (cosmological=1; tested at level 3 for the first 20
  steps only): comoving ICs. a_begin makes the mean matter density equal to
  Omega_m rho_crit_0 (a_begin = 0.0136, z = 72, with the defaults). The box
  follows the Hubble flow, so the physics is not that of the default run.
  SWIFT stops on zero velocities in cosmological runs, so the ICs get
  random velocities (--velocity-sigma, default 1 km/s). makeIC.py writes
  the Cosmology block, the comoving softenings, the comoving h_max and the
  output times to zoom_runtime_params.txt.

Parameter files: params_sinks.yml (--sinks) and params_star_formation.yml
(--star-formation). The physics blocks are the ones of
examples/SinkParticles/HomogeneousBox/params.yml.
